# Privacy And Anonymization Rules

## Example Data
- When an operator supplies real examples (documents, files, records,
  screenshots, or sample data) for analysis, treat personal and identifying
  values as private.
- Real samples may be used locally for analysis or validation. They must
  never be committed to or copied into the repository, and must not be
  quoted by name in repository content or git history.

## Canonical Person Names
- When examples need named persons, assign canonical names in alphabetical
  order from the fixed per-letter list: the first anonymized person is
  Angela, the second Bert, the third Catherine, and so on through the
  alphabet.
- Canonical list: A Angela, B Bert, C Catherine, D Dennis, E Emma, F Felix,
  G Grace, H Hannah, I Iris, J Jonas, K Katrien, L Laurens, M Marie,
  N Niels, O Olivier, P Petra, Q Quinten, R Renée, S Simon, T Thomas,
  U Ursula, V Vincent, W Wouter, X Xander, Y Yasmine, Z Zoë.
- Reuse the same letter-to-name assignment consistently across features,
  specs, fixtures, and tests. Never assign the same canonical name to two
  different real persons within one example set.
- Use neutral entity placeholders such as `Acme` for companies.

## Generated Content
- Before writing specifications, Gherkin, QA procedures, tests, fixtures,
  task documents, commit messages, handoff drafts, or generated artifacts,
  anonymize every personal or identifying value.
- Use canonical placeholder names and neutral entity names (for example
  `Angela`, `Bert`, or `Acme`) consistently across examples, specs, and
  tests.
- Do not reproduce real names, file names that contain them, email
  addresses, phone numbers, addresses, approver names, or other identifying
  details in any file or commit.
- When a real sample is needed only for validation, refer to it by a
  generic pattern (for example `Timesheet_<name>_<M>_<yyyy>.pdf` under an
  inbox directory) and keep the real file outside the repository.

## Commits And Handoffs
- Before committing or sending a handoff, check the diff and history for
  personal identifiers.
- If personal data was committed, rewrite the commit(s) to remove it, prune
  the old objects, and re-verify before continuing.
- Never send handoff content that contains personal identifiers from real
  examples.
